After performing a mutation in a Server Action, you can refresh the current page to show the latest state by calling `refresh` from `next/cache` [4]. 

```ts filename="app/lib/actions.ts"
'use server'

import { auth } from '@/lib/auth'
import { refresh } from 'next/cache'

export async function updatePost(formData: FormData) {
  // Mutate data
  // ...

  refresh()
}
```

To refresh cached data or revalidate the Next.js cache, you can also call `revalidatePath` or `revalidateTag` within the Server Function [1, 3]. Note that `refresh()` does not revalidate tagged data; use `updateTag` or `revalidateTag` for that purpose [4].

To have every page title automatically include a site name as a suffix, you can use `title.template` within the `metadata` object in your root layout [3]. 

You must define a template string containing `%s` (which represents the child segment's title) alongside a `default` title [3]. For example:

```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx"
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: {
    template: '%s | Acme',
    default: 'Acme',
  },
}
```

You can handle unexpected runtime errors and display a fallback UI by using an `error.js` file [1]. 

`error.js` wraps a route segment and its nested children in a React Error Boundary [1]. When an error is thrown within that boundary, the `error` component is rendered as the fallback UI [1].

```tsx filename="app/dashboard/error.tsx"
'use client'

export default function Error({
  error,
  retry,
}: {
  error: unknown
  retry: () => void
}) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

You can build a JSON API endpoint inside the `app` directory by defining a Route Handler using a `route.js` or `route.ts` file [3, 5]. 

Route Handlers allow you to create custom request handlers for a given route using the Web Request and Response APIs [3].

```ts filename="app/api/route.ts"
export async function GET(request: Request) {
  return Response.json({ message: 'Hello World' })
}
```

To make a component run in the client so it can use features like `useState` and event handlers such as `onClick`, you need to declare it as a Client Component [1, 2]. 

To do this, add the `'use client'` directive at the very top of the file, before any imports [1]. The props passed to this component must be serializable [1].

```tsx filename="app/components/counter.tsx"
'use client'

import { useState } from 'react'

export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

You can disable prefetching for specific routes by setting the `prefetch` prop to `false` on the `<Link>` component [1, 3]. For example, you can create a custom link component for your footer like this:

```tsx
'use client'

import Link, { LinkProps } from 'next/link'

function NoPrefetchLink({
  prefetch,
  ...rest
}: LinkProps & { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <Link {...rest} prefetch={false} />
}
```

You can read a cookie value in a Server Component by using the `cookies` function from `next/headers`. 

```tsx
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'

export default async function Page() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies()
  const sessionCookie = cookieStore.get('session-id')?.value

  return <p>Session: {sessionCookie}</p>
}
```

If you are using Cache Components and the component accesses request data like cookies, it must be wrapped in a `<Suspense>` boundary so the rest of the page can be prerendered into a static shell [1].

To prerender every blog post page at build time in the Pages router, use `getStaticPaths` to fetch the list of posts and return their paths with `fallback: false`, along with `getStaticProps` to fetch the individual post data [1, 2].

```jsx filename="pages/posts/[id].js"
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const res = await fetch('https://.../posts')
  const posts = await res.json()

  const paths = posts.map((post) => ({
    params: { id: post.id },
  }))

  return { paths, fallback: false }
}

export async function getStaticProps({ params }) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://.../posts/${params.id}`)
  const post = await res.json()

  return { props: { post } }
}

export default function Post({ post }) {
  return <article><h1>{post.title}</h1></article>
}
```

In the App router, use `generateStaticParams` to provide sample param values and prerender the routes at build time [3].

You can render multiple independent sections, such as team and analytics, at the same time using **Parallel Routes** [1]. Parallel routes are created using named **slots** with the `@folder` convention (e.g., `@team` and `@analytics`) [2].

Slots are passed as props to the shared parent layout component, allowing you to render them in parallel alongside the standard `children` prop [2]:

```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx"
export default function Layout({
  children,
  team,
  analytics,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
  analytics: React.ReactNode
  team: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    <>
      {children}
      {team}
      {analytics}
    </>
  )
}
```[2]

To forward requests to another backend (such as `/api` requests), you can use `rewrites` in your `next.config.js` or `next.config.ts` file [1, 2]. 

```ts filename="next.config.ts"
import { NextConfig } from 'next'

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  async rewrites() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        destination: 'https://your-backend.com/:path*',
      },
    ]
  },
}
```[2]

To navigate to another page from a button click handler, you can use the `push` method from the `useRouter` hook [2]. 

```tsx filename="app/page.tsx"
'use client'

import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation'

export default function Page() {
  const router = useRouter()

  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={() => router.push('/dashboard')}>
      Dashboard
    </button>
  )
}
```

If you do not need to programmatically navigate the user, you should use a `<Link>` component instead [2].

In the Pages Router, you can fetch fresh data on every request by exporting an `async` function called `getServerSideProps` from your page [4]. 

This function is called by the server on every request, allowing you to fetch data and pass it to your page via props [4].

```jsx
export default function Page({ data }) {
  // Render data...
}

export async function getServerSideProps() {
  const res = await fetch(`https://.../data`)
  const data = await res.json()

  return { props: { data } }
}
```

In the Pages Router, you can rebuild a static page in the background using Incremental Static Regeneration by returning the `revalidate` property set to `60` (seconds) inside your `getStaticProps` function [1, 5]. 

```js
export async function getStaticProps() {
  const res = await fetch('https://.../posts')
  const posts = await res.json()

  return {
    props: {
      posts,
    },
    // Re-generate the page at most once every 60 seconds
    revalidate: 60,
  }
}
```[1]

You can run tasks like logging or analytics after a response is finished by using the `after` function. It accepts a callback that executes after the response or prerender has completed without blocking the response [2].

`after` can be used in Server Components, Server Functions, Route Handlers, and Proxy [2]. Here is an example of using it in a layout:

```tsx filename="app/layout.tsx" switcher
import { after } from 'next/server'
// Custom logging function
import { log } from '@/app/utils'

export default function Layout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  after(() => {
    // Execute after the layout is rendered and sent to the user
    log()
  })
  return <>{children}</>
}
```

Additionally, in Proxy, the second parameter (`NextFetchEvent`) exposes a `waitUntil(promise)` method that keeps the invocation alive so background tasks like logging or analytics can finish after the response is sent [4].

To load a third-party script only when the browser is idle, you can use the `lazyOnload` strategy with the `next/script` component [1, 4]. 

```jsx filename="app/page.js"
import Script from 'next/script'

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <Script src="https://example.com/script.js" strategy="lazyOnload" />
    </>
  )
}
```

Scripts using this strategy are injected client-side during browser idle time and load after all other page resources have been fetched [4].

In the App Router, metadata objects exported from multiple segments in the same route are shallowly merged together, where duplicate keys are replaced based on their ordering [2]. You can set the `title` and `description` fields directly within a metadata object exported from a page or layout [2].

For example:
```jsx filename="app/blog/page.js"
export const metadata = {
  title: 'Blog',
  description: 'Acme is a...',
}
```

If your metadata relies on external data under Cache Components, you can also fetch and cache it inside `generateMetadata` using `'use cache'` [3]:

```tsx filename="app/page.tsx"
export async function generateMetadata() {
  'use cache'
  const { title, description } = await db.query('site-metadata')
  return { title, description }
}
```

Yes, many webpack loaders work with Turbopack, and several have been tested to work successfully (such as `@svgr/webpack` and `svg-inline-loader`) [1, 2]. 

You can configure them in `next.config.js` under the `turbopack.rules` option by mapping file extensions to the desired loaders [2]:

```js filename="next.config.js"
module.exports = {
  turbopack: {
    rules: {
      '*.svg': {
        loaders: ['@svgr/webpack'],
        as: '*.js',
      },
    },
  },
}
```

Note that Turbopack implements a core subset of the webpack loader API, only supports loaders that return JavaScript code, and requires options to be plain JavaScript primitives, objects, or arrays [2].
